# Compound bond: issuer classification and settlement

Status: internally checked conditional illustration; actual instrument
classification is not established. MC17, MC19; gaps G4 and G7.

## Facts and proposed answer

The issuer receives 100, owes two annual coupons of 5 and principal 100, and
the holder may convert that principal into ten ordinary shares. Currency is
the issuer's functional currency. Assume no fees, other options, contingencies
or settlement alternatives; assume the share-conversion feature qualifies as
equity. The market yield for otherwise comparable non-convertible debt is
assumed to be 10%, not estimated from the example.

Under those conditions the proposed issuer split is liability
`5/1.1 + 105/1.1² = 11050/121`, and residual equity `1050/121`.
The exact calculation keeps the components summing to 100. Using the stated
effective-interest schedule, liability after coupons one and two is `1050/11`
and 100. Interest expense totals `2260/121`, including discount accretion.

Issue: debit cash 100; credit liability `11050/121` and conversion equity
`1050/121`. Accrual debits interest expense and credits liability; each coupon
debits liability and credits cash 5. At maturity, redemption debits liability
and credits cash 100. Conversion instead debits liability and credits issued
share equity 100; the original equity component remains in equity. The
[checked journal report](../accounting-dossier-checks.json) preserves both
settlement paths. A 5% comparable yield gives liability 100 as an arithmetic
boundary case; it is not evidence that a real conversion option has zero value.

## Source and boundary

[AASB 132](../../research/standards/aasb132.pdf), pp. 12–14 and 25–26:
paragraph 22 describes fixed-number/fixed-amount settlement; 25–27 address
contingencies and settlement choices; 28–32 require the compound split and
residual; AG30 confines this treatment to the issuer, AG31 supplies comparable
liability discounting, AG32 supplies no maturity-conversion gain/loss, and
AG33–AG35 distinguish early repurchase and induced conversion. Paragraph 31
refers subsequent financial-liability measurement to AASB 9. This dossier's
accretion calculation is a declared schedule; it does not fill the missing
primary measurement-source audit by implication.

The PDF's pp. 1 and 5 say the compilation applies to annual periods beginning
on/after 1 January 2023 and **before 1 July 2026**, incorporating amendments
through 15 December 2022. Period start determines that condition; a calendar
2026 annual period is inside it. The local source is an official Australian
IAS-equivalent text with the comparison qualifications on p. 4, not proof of
every jurisdiction's currently adopted version.

## Strongest counterargument and remaining internal work

A variable share count, cash option, foreign-currency exercise amount, put,
reset clause, contingent payment or related guarantee could defeat the assumed
classification. The arithmetic can remain perfectly balanced while the
classification is wrong. Do not apply this issuer example to the holder.

For an actual bond, first retain the complete contract and side agreements,
prepare a clause-by-clause settlement table, establish reporting basis and
period, acquire the applicable primary measurement authority, document the
comparable yield and test early settlement/modification where relevant.
US-GAAP and ASBE classification need their own dated sources. All are internal
preparation tasks; do not pay for a reviewer to discover the missing clauses.

A later review can ask whether named clauses X/Y change the proposed equity
classification under a cited paragraph, with both proposed treatments and
their reconciled entries supplied. No actual clause requiring that judgment
has been supplied in the current synthetic example.
